ef 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Exponential Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1033 391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4f6afc08-f23d-50b8-a5e5-a6052d6e4308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050828 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050828 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050828 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050828 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050828 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050828 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050828 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Equations With Even Exponents Using the Nth Root Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050828 #edb/ref 1033 1587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050828 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "69d7c525-79e4-514e-80ba-739018d9e137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147226 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2175" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147226 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose fourth term is $2$ and whose seventh term is $-686.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147226 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d8383ad-c617-5b01-8055-214a8c639f12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147226 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms $a_m$ and $a_n,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given $a_7 = -686$ and $a_4 = 2.$ Substituting $n=7$ and $m=4$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{7-4} & =\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{4}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{-686}{2} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =-343.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by solving the equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3} & =-343 \\\\ {r}^{3} & =(-7)^{3} \\\\ r & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147229 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2178" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147229 #edb/ref 1071 "The third term of a geometric sequence is $80$ and its fifth term is $20.$  Find the common ratio $r$ of this sequence given that $r$ is positive." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147229 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7aa4b1ba-4362-5a4b-88d4-25d9ebe2cef7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147229 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms $a_m$ and $a_n,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given $a_5 = 20$ and $a_3 = 80.$ Substituting $n=5$ and $m=3$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{5-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{20}{80} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147230 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2179" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147230 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation for the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose $4$th term is $6$ and whose $9$th term is $192.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147230 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "84364858-da8e-5241-85ca-ec84057bc6e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147230 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1r^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=4$ and $n=9,$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{4-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{3}, \\\\ {a}_{9} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{9-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $a_4 = 6$ and $a_9 = 192,$ we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}{r}^{3}=6 \\\\ {a}_{1}{r}^{8}=192\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by dividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{r}^{8}}{{a}_{1}{r}^{3}} & =\\frac{192}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{\\,{a}_{1}\\,}{r}^{8}}{\\cancel{\\,{a}_{1}\\,}{r}^{3}} & =\\frac{192}{6} \\\\ \\frac{{r}^{8}}{{r}^{3}} & =32 \\\\ {r}^{5} & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio satisfies $r^5 = 32.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147461 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147461 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147461 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186148112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147461 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186148113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147461 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147461 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147461 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Equation for the Common Ratio" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147461 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147461 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "81a78a4d-f4ef-5c51-b34e-22d3ee51c7c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147462 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147462 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147462 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147462 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147462 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186148111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147462 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147462 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147462 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Common Ratio Using a Formula Given Two Non-Conse